00:07:36.064 --> 00:07:37.953
- Coming in at number two is

00:07:37.953 --> 00:07:38.994
The Tipping Point:

00:07:38.994 --> 00:07:41.793
How Little Things Can
Make A Big Difference by

00:07:41.793 --> 00:07:43.692
Malcolm Gladwell.

00:07:43.692 --> 00:07:46.355
(guitar music)

00:07:46.355 --> 00:07:49.779
- A couple years ago, I was
covering the AIDS epidemic.

00:07:49.779 --> 00:07:50.972
So I got very interested in

00:07:50.972 --> 00:07:53.536
the internal dynamics of epidemics,

00:07:53.536 --> 00:07:56.056
because their own, sort of weird logic.

00:07:56.056 --> 00:07:58.069
And it just began to occur to me that

00:07:58.069 --> 00:07:59.334
I didn't understand why,

00:07:59.334 --> 00:08:01.463
when we talk about contagious things,

00:08:01.463 --> 00:08:05.771
we confine our conversation
to viruses or to diseases,

00:08:05.771 --> 00:08:06.702
because clearly--

00:08:06.702 --> 00:08:08.079
- The phenomena of disease
could be attributed

00:08:08.079 --> 00:08:08.990
to other places,

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.331
to other ideas.

00:08:10.331 --> 00:08:11.356
- Yes, exactly.

00:08:11.356 --> 00:08:13.099
Those same principles, I mean,

00:08:13.099 --> 00:08:15.068
so many different things or other things,

00:08:15.068 --> 00:08:17.376
ideas, trends, song lyrics,

00:08:17.376 --> 00:08:19.711
are contagious in precisely the same way.

00:08:19.711 --> 00:08:22.523
I mean, I talk in the book
about the word "yawn".

00:08:22.523 --> 00:08:25.265
If I say the word "yawn" long
enough you will start yawning,

00:08:25.265 --> 00:08:28.191
and people watching this
show will start yawning.

00:08:28.191 --> 00:08:31.430
That's an incredibly contagious word.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.753
And it's contagious in
precisely the same way as--

00:08:33.753 --> 00:08:35.158
- Contagion meaning everybody catches it

00:08:35.158 --> 00:08:37.160
or contagious--?
- Everyone's catching it and

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:40.904
it's spreading from one source everywhere.

00:08:40.904 --> 00:08:43.370
The same thing is true for, I think,

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:47.059
for products or ideas or
behaviors, especially.

00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:49.884
I talk in the book about all
kinds of contagious behaviors.

00:08:49.884 --> 00:08:53.432
Well, the tipping point
is the word that comes

00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:55.077
from the study of epidemics,

00:08:55.077 --> 00:08:57.513
is the describe that
moment in the epidemic

00:08:57.513 --> 00:08:58.930
when it explodes.

00:08:59.865 --> 00:09:02.608
The moment of critical mass.

00:09:02.608 --> 00:09:03.976
And if you look at every epidemic,

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.162
there is always that moment

00:09:05.162 --> 00:09:07.800
when the curve suddenly
shoots up very sharply,

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:08.886
and dramatically.

00:09:08.886 --> 00:09:10.536
And so understanding how you can get

00:09:10.536 --> 00:09:12.926
to the tipping point is really this,

00:09:12.926 --> 00:09:14.324
is the critical question.

00:09:14.324 --> 00:09:18.552
The word-of-mouth epidemics
are something that

00:09:18.552 --> 00:09:20.160
I spend a lot of time on in the book.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.346
People use that phrase without
ever saying what they mean.

00:09:23.346 --> 00:09:24.631
I hear it all the time.

00:09:24.631 --> 00:09:26.045
So-and-so is great, what about--

00:09:26.045 --> 00:09:28.165
And I had never read an explanation,

00:09:28.165 --> 00:09:29.244
an actually attempt to pin down

00:09:29.244 --> 00:09:30.827
what that phenomenon is.

00:09:30.827 --> 00:09:32.580
If there is such a thing as word-of-mouth,

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:33.413
who starts it?

00:09:33.413 --> 00:09:34.441
Who sustains it?

00:09:34.441 --> 00:09:35.476
Who creates it?

00:09:35.476 --> 00:09:36.385
So I decided--

00:09:36.385 --> 00:09:37.360
- [Interviewer] Did somebody start it?

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.370
- Oh yeah, absolutely.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.194
The whole first part of the
book is an attempt to say

00:09:41.194 --> 00:09:43.450
"Alright, this personality is responsible

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:44.882
"for starting word-of-mouth."

00:09:44.882 --> 00:09:47.200
This personality, and
I have profiles of each

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:48.987
of these kinds of people, is responsible

00:09:48.987 --> 00:09:49.820
for spreading it.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:51.177
This is the kind of person.

00:09:51.177 --> 00:09:53.160
Because it comes directly from,

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.963
it's and idea borrowed
directly from disease.

00:09:55.963 --> 00:09:57.265
From diseases.

00:09:57.265 --> 00:09:58.432
If you look at disease epidemics,

00:09:58.432 --> 00:09:59.678
they are started and spread

00:09:59.678 --> 00:10:02.207
by a tiny, tiny group
of exceptional people.

00:10:02.207 --> 00:10:03.695
That same idea, I think,

00:10:03.695 --> 00:10:05.862
is a really powerful way of understanding

00:10:05.862 --> 00:10:09.022
this previously amorphous
concept of word-of-mouth.

